1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,356
The app that helped Starbucks collect over 1.7

2
00:00:02,356 --> 00:00:05,240
billion dollars in customer balances also drove customers away.

3
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:07,888
From July to September 2024,

4
00:00:07,888 --> 00:00:09,222
transactions at U.S.

5
00:00:09,222 --> 00:00:12,290
Starbucks stores fell 10% from a year earlier.

6
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:15,150
Nine customers for every ten who used to come.

7
00:00:15,930 --> 00:00:18,916
If you own Starbucks stock or are thinking about it,

8
00:00:18,916 --> 00:00:21,386
the next nine minutes lay out how Starbucks

9
00:00:21,386 --> 00:00:24,200
makes money and what today's price is betting on.

10
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:27,500
Here's what you'll take away.

11
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:30,331
One: what the 1.7 billion dollars customers

12
00:00:30,331 --> 00:00:32,180
left with Starbucks really is.

13
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:34,406
Two: why customers stopped coming,

14
00:00:34,406 --> 00:00:35,790
and how they came back.

15
00:00:35,970 --> 00:00:39,750
Three: the three numbers to check on October 29 earnings.

16
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:43,816
Here's the strange part: this app was the best

17
00:00:43,816 --> 00:00:46,030
money-maker Starbucks ever had.

18
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:50,300
First, how much money did this app collect?

19
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:53,566
An American commuter orders ahead in the app

20
00:00:53,566 --> 00:00:55,600
and just grabs the cup at the store.

21
00:00:55,770 --> 00:00:57,144
When the balance runs low,

22
00:00:57,144 --> 00:00:59,310
the app reloads 25 dollars automatically.

23
00:01:00,090 --> 00:01:02,962
35.8 million active Starbucks Rewards members in the U.S.

24
00:01:02,962 --> 00:01:04,020
use the app this way.

25
00:01:04,210 --> 00:01:06,995
They account for 59% of spending at U.S.

26
00:01:06,995 --> 00:01:08,666
company-operated stores.

27
00:01:08,666 --> 00:01:11,660
Six of every ten dollars come from members.

28
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:13,591
In one quarter,

29
00:01:13,591 --> 00:01:17,810
3.1 billion dollars were loaded onto cards and the app.

30
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:21,720
The money reaches Starbucks before the coffee is even made.

31
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:26,457
Money loaded but not yet spent comes to about

32
00:01:26,457 --> 00:01:29,264
1.75 billion dollars in the U.S.

33
00:01:29,264 --> 00:01:29,790
alone.

34
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:31,847
Right after the holiday gift season,

35
00:01:31,847 --> 00:01:33,210
it tops 2 billion dollars.

36
00:01:33,990 --> 00:01:35,314
In accounting terms,

37
00:01:35,314 --> 00:01:38,623
this balance is a debt Starbucks repays in coffee.

38
00:01:38,623 --> 00:01:42,130
It takes the money first and serves the coffee later.

39
00:01:42,310 --> 00:01:45,955
But this debt carries no interest and no due date.

40
00:01:45,955 --> 00:01:48,361
If a bank offered me those terms,

41
00:01:48,361 --> 00:01:49,600
I'd borrow today.

42
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:53,251
So does Starbucks earn big interest on this money?

43
00:01:53,251 --> 00:01:55,490
The answer is smaller than you'd think.

44
00:01:56,270 --> 00:01:57,177
In fiscal 2025,

45
00:01:57,177 --> 00:01:59,474
Starbucks made 2.94 billion dollars of

46
00:01:59,474 --> 00:02:01,710
operating income from selling coffee.

47
00:02:01,890 --> 00:02:04,704
Interest and other income from all of its cash

48
00:02:04,704 --> 00:02:06,416
came to 113 million dollars,

49
00:02:06,416 --> 00:02:08,190
about 4% of operating income.

50
00:02:08,370 --> 00:02:10,313
Customers can spend the balance any time,

51
00:02:10,313 --> 00:02:12,920
so Starbucks can't lock it up in long-term investments.

52
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,332
The books show one more line,

53
00:02:15,332 --> 00:02:17,076
bigger than interest: breakage,

54
00:02:17,076 --> 00:02:20,058
the balances Starbucks estimates will never be spent,

55
00:02:20,058 --> 00:02:21,071
booked as revenue.

56
00:02:21,071 --> 00:02:23,040
222 million dollars in fiscal 2025.

57
00:02:23,220 --> 00:02:26,577
Forgotten gift cards earned twice as much as interest.

58
00:02:26,577 --> 00:02:28,940
Honestly, I have one in my drawer too.

59
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:32,030
Still, breakage is under 8% of operating income.

60
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:36,335
So the first answer: the balance isn't money that earns interest.

61
00:02:36,335 --> 00:02:38,390
It's money that brings customers back.

62
00:02:38,570 --> 00:02:40,404
With 12 dollars left in the app,

63
00:02:40,404 --> 00:02:43,099
it's hard to try another cafe tomorrow morning.

64
00:02:43,099 --> 00:02:44,360
That's called lock-in.

65
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:47,480
The problem is, the app worked too well.

66
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:50,741
Picture the pickup counter at a U.S.

67
00:02:50,741 --> 00:02:52,050
Starbucks at 8 a.m.

68
00:02:52,830 --> 00:02:55,527
Cups for app orders line the pickup counter

69
00:02:55,527 --> 00:02:58,600
while customers at the register wait behind them.

70
00:02:58,770 --> 00:03:00,090
Today one in three orders at U.S.

71
00:03:00,090 --> 00:03:01,850
company-operated stores comes in through the

72
00:03:01,850 --> 00:03:03,130
app before the customer arrives.

73
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:07,520
App orders often come with add-ons like extra syrup or cold foam.

74
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:10,870
Each add-on raises what Starbucks earns per cup.

75
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:14,410
But it also stretches the time a barista needs to make each drink.

76
00:03:15,190 --> 00:03:16,017
In April 2024,

77
00:03:16,017 --> 00:03:18,734
the then-CEO admitted Starbucks was struggling

78
00:03:18,734 --> 00:03:20,565
to keep up with morning demand,

79
00:03:20,565 --> 00:03:23,400
and long waits were turning some customers away.

80
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:26,510
Founder Howard Schultz went as far as calling

81
00:03:26,510 --> 00:03:29,180
the app Starbucks' biggest Achilles heel.

82
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:33,506
Transactions fell 6% in April to June 2024,

83
00:03:33,506 --> 00:03:35,980
then 10% in July to September.

84
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:41,240
The CEO who took over in March 2023 was replaced after 17 months.

85
00:03:42,010 --> 00:03:45,475
This isn't the first time Starbucks lost customers.

86
00:03:45,475 --> 00:03:48,056
On February 26, 2008, about 7,000 U.S.

87
00:03:48,056 --> 00:03:50,230
Starbucks stores closed at once.

88
00:03:50,410 --> 00:03:53,452
Founder Howard Schultz shut them for three and

89
00:03:53,452 --> 00:03:56,890
a half hours to retrain baristas on making espresso.

90
00:03:57,070 --> 00:03:58,792
Back then the cause was the coffee.

91
00:03:58,792 --> 00:04:01,300
This time it was stores overloaded with app orders.

92
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:02,866
So part two,

93
00:04:02,866 --> 00:04:06,470
the cause: stores slowed down by a flood of app orders.

94
00:04:06,650 --> 00:04:09,710
How did Starbucks bring customers back?

95
00:04:10,490 --> 00:04:12,146
On August 13, 2024,

96
00:04:12,146 --> 00:04:15,720
Starbucks named Brian Niccol its new CEO.

97
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:19,528
Niccol had spent six years growing the burrito

98
00:04:19,528 --> 00:04:21,240
chain Chipotle as its CEO.

99
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:24,630
Starbucks shares jumped 24% intraday on the news,

100
00:04:24,630 --> 00:04:27,970
the biggest intraday gain in the company's history.

101
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:31,018
His work arrangement made headlines too: he

102
00:04:31,018 --> 00:04:33,381
would fly a company jet more than 1,000 miles

103
00:04:33,381 --> 00:04:35,167
between his home in Newport Beach,

104
00:04:35,167 --> 00:04:37,110
California, and Seattle headquarters.

105
00:04:37,290 --> 00:04:40,051
I complain about two subway stops.

106
00:04:40,051 --> 00:04:42,000
His commute is a flight.

107
00:04:42,770 --> 00:04:44,452
Instead of adding new drinks,

108
00:04:44,452 --> 00:04:47,120
Niccol announced he would cut 30% of the menu.

109
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:50,611
Customers staying in got ceramic mugs again,

110
00:04:50,611 --> 00:04:54,600
and baristas started handwriting notes on to-go cups.

111
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:58,150
He also set a goal: every drink in four minutes.

112
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:00,591
Niccol didn't kill the app.

113
00:05:00,591 --> 00:05:03,283
He focused on making stores fast again after

114
00:05:03,283 --> 00:05:05,240
app orders had slowed them down.

115
00:05:06,010 --> 00:05:07,169
It wasn't free.

116
00:05:07,169 --> 00:05:10,645
Spending on staff and stores first pushed the

117
00:05:10,645 --> 00:05:13,890
fiscal 2025 operating margin down to 7.9%.

118
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:14,383
U.S.

119
00:05:14,383 --> 00:05:18,090
comparable store sales fell 2% that year as well.

120
00:05:18,870 --> 00:05:21,538
Then, from April to June this year, U.S.

121
00:05:21,538 --> 00:05:23,740
comparable store sales rose 7.9%.

122
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:26,200
What matters is why: transactions,

123
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:28,950
the number of customer visits, rose 4.2%.

124
00:05:29,730 --> 00:05:33,038
Active Rewards members grew from 33.8 million

125
00:05:33,038 --> 00:05:35,170
to 35.8 million in two years.

126
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:39,380
Not just higher prices: more people are coming back to the stores.

127
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:44,814
That's the answer to part two: once the stores got fast again,

128
00:05:44,814 --> 00:05:46,240
customers returned.

129
00:05:47,030 --> 00:05:50,040
So how did it go for Starbucks shareholders?

130
00:05:50,820 --> 00:05:54,239
If you had bought 100 million won of Starbucks

131
00:05:54,239 --> 00:05:56,098
near its peak on July 23,

132
00:05:56,098 --> 00:05:56,469
2021,

133
00:05:56,469 --> 00:05:59,740
it would be worth 8천만 원 at the Oct 23 close.

134
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:02,563
Bought the day before the Niccol news,

135
00:06:02,563 --> 00:06:03,884
on August 12, 2024,

136
00:06:03,884 --> 00:06:07,570
the same 100 million won would be worth 1억 3천만 원 now.

137
00:06:07,750 --> 00:06:08,432
Same company,

138
00:06:08,432 --> 00:06:11,160
very different results depending on when you bought.

139
00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:15,130
So how much of the recovery is already in today's price?

140
00:06:15,910 --> 00:06:18,732
Let's borrow the method of Warren Buffett's teacher,

141
00:06:18,732 --> 00:06:19,600
Benjamin Graham.

142
00:06:19,780 --> 00:06:21,986
Take average earnings over several years and

143
00:06:21,986 --> 00:06:23,290
pay at most 10 times that.

144
00:06:23,470 --> 00:06:26,426
Count future growth as zero.

145
00:06:26,426 --> 00:06:29,170
It's a strict floor value.

146
00:06:29,950 --> 00:06:33,578
By this math one Starbucks share is worth $26.16.

147
00:06:33,578 --> 00:06:36,540
At the Oct 23 close it traded at $100.8.

148
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:39,155
The market pays 3.9x the floor value.

149
00:06:39,155 --> 00:06:42,379
In our Tesla episode the same math gave 38 times,

150
00:06:42,379 --> 00:06:44,880
so Starbucks looks far less expensive.

151
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:48,248
So what is the ValueNote score of a stock that

152
00:06:48,248 --> 00:06:49,430
looks less expensive?

153
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:54,300
Starbucks scores 21 out of 100 on ValueNote.

154
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:57,909
The main reason is earnings: fiscal 2025

155
00:06:57,909 --> 00:07:01,510
earnings per share fell by more than half.

156
00:07:01,690 --> 00:07:04,926
The weak year spent fixing stores is dragging

157
00:07:04,926 --> 00:07:06,940
down the multi-year average.

158
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:11,187
The company guided fiscal 2026 adjusted EPS to

159
00:07:11,187 --> 00:07:12,770
2.55 to 2.65 dollars.

160
00:07:12,950 --> 00:07:16,165
That's more than 20% above last year's 2.13.

161
00:07:16,165 --> 00:07:19,600
This report is the first check on that outlook.

162
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:21,924
If the recovery continues,

163
00:07:21,924 --> 00:07:23,944
more customers become more profit,

164
00:07:23,944 --> 00:07:25,667
the multi-year average rises,

165
00:07:25,667 --> 00:07:27,330
and so does the floor value.

166
00:07:27,510 --> 00:07:28,362
If it stalls,

167
00:07:28,362 --> 00:07:31,310
the weak years keep dragging the average down

168
00:07:31,310 --> 00:07:33,800
and the gap with today's price widens.

169
00:07:34,580 --> 00:07:37,753
ValueNote runs this calculation daily on more than 600 companies,

170
00:07:37,753 --> 00:07:38,680
Starbucks included.

171
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:41,848
It's a floor that counts growth as zero,

172
00:07:41,848 --> 00:07:45,060
not a target price or a buy or sell signal.

173
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:47,232
Finally, part three.

174
00:07:47,232 --> 00:07:49,458
Starbucks reports after the U.S.

175
00:07:49,458 --> 00:07:50,850
close on October 29.

176
00:07:51,630 --> 00:07:53,823
First, U.S. comparable store sales.

177
00:07:53,823 --> 00:07:56,830
The company guided to 6.5% or more this quarter.

178
00:07:57,010 --> 00:07:57,448
Second,

179
00:07:57,448 --> 00:08:00,705
transactions: whether the number of customer visits,

180
00:08:00,705 --> 00:08:02,270
not prices, keeps rising.

181
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:03,880
Third, operating margin.

182
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:06,443
The company expects above 11% for the year,

183
00:08:06,443 --> 00:08:09,900
showing whether the store spending is turning into profit.

184
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:14,083
In 2008 Schultz closed the stores to retrain on espresso.

185
00:08:14,083 --> 00:08:16,053
In 2024 Niccol brought back mugs,

186
00:08:16,053 --> 00:08:18,500
handwritten notes and a four-minute goal.

187
00:08:18,690 --> 00:08:21,932
Both fixed the in-store experience first.

188
00:08:21,932 --> 00:08:24,779
Whether customer visits keep rising,

189
00:08:24,779 --> 00:08:26,440
October 29 will tell.

190
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:29,623
And one tip for your own money: load only what

191
00:08:29,623 --> 00:08:31,189
you'll spend in a month.

192
00:08:31,189 --> 00:08:33,670
The interest goes to Starbucks anyway.

193
